{
  "ideas": [
    {
      "id": 11,
      "name": "Two Roads",
      "approach": "handmade",
      "approachWhy": "The fun is a clear, fair trade-off that you can see from the fork, so every split is tuned by hand. A generator could easily make a 'short' road that is not really faster, or a 'safe' road that is not really safe.",
      "engine": {
        "have": [
          "BUILD.TwoWays already splits the lane with a see-through divider (look 'pipe'): safe jelly steps on one side, beams on the other",
          "slab/prop helpers, 'jelly', 'rope', 'pad', 'gate', 'truss' (climbable) and 'ghost' (no collision) parts",
          "flip motion (solid on a duty cycle) for a gate that opens and shuts",
          "slide motion for platforms that move",
          "the server sends you back to the start once you drop under the lowest platform you could still land on, so a lower road can catch you",
          "tests: GAP_MAX 6, RISE_MAX 3.5 between steps, and jumpUnderRoof for jumps under a low roof",
          "ObbyService already tells you your run time when you clear the course",
          "bounce pad (orange spring pad) and the melt motion (MeltingTiles: drops a set time after you land)"
        ],
        "new": [
          {
            "what": "Signpost prop at each fork: stopwatch icon = short and hard, turtle icon = long and safe, built from parts or a decal (if it uses a SurfaceGui it needs an explicit MaxDistance and a world-labels registry row), plus a sound as you pass it",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Route-aware tests: tag each part with a route ('fast' / 'safe' / 'secret') and run the gap/rise and time checks on every route, not only one; also check the fast road really is quicker. Same branch idea as Key Run's branch-aware tests (idea 20), so build it once",
            "size": "M"
          },
          {
            "what": "'Time saved' popup where the roads join (+2.4 s on the fast road), on the client",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Hazard touch = back to the start pad and the red goo look: the shared piece from Crusher Alley (idea 1), not a second one",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Timed gate on the safe road: flip motion on a 'gate' wall, its glow and tick hung on Beat Pads' BeatClock (idea 18) instead of a timer of its own",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Gate that shuts for good a few seconds after YOU enter the section: a per-player section clock on the client. Crusher Alley's speed-up controller (1), Rising Goo's flood (12) and Collapsing Bridge's collapse clock (24) all start a clock when the local player steps in; build one 'section entry clock' and share it",
            "size": "M"
          },
          {
            "what": "Hidden shortcut ledge: a near-invisible solid part with a faint sparkle and a scratched arrow (Secret Ledge only)",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Cracked trap tile for the drop-down shortcut: reuse Melting Floor's melt motion with its Melt warning look (cracks, darkening, crack sound; idea 2), so the shortcut reads by the shared 'about to fall' look instead of a ghost tile",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Stacked roads: check the fall-back-to-start height uses the lower road when you fall off the upper one",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "Speed strip on one fast road (Boost Gap): reuse Speed Strip's Client boost controller and boost test rule (idea 16)",
            "size": "S"
          },
          {
            "what": "A best-time record (or a medal for a fast clear) so the short road is worth the risk. Today you only see your time once",
            "size": "M"
          }
        ]
      },
      "readability": "Every fork has a signpost (stopwatch = short and hard, turtle = long and safe), and the divider between roads is see-through, so you can study both before you pick. The fast road has fewer, longer jumps, and its hazards use the shared red goo look; the safe road has wide jelly steps and short hops. A drop-down shortcut uses the shared cracked, darkening look, and a speed-strip shortcut uses the yellow chevrons, so no hidden piece needs a new look except the sparkle ledge.",
      "generator": null,
